course: dictionary
lesson: 11814
title: Bigger and comparing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bigger is used to compare two things. It means larger in size or amount than another thing. «My elephant is bigger than my dog.» You are not told how big either animal is; you learn only that the elephant is the larger one.
explain: Bigger can also mean older or more serious. «My bigger brother is fifteen» means he is older than you. «This problem is bigger than we thought» means it is more serious than we first believed. Check the other words in the sentence to see which meaning fits.
explain: Big is the basic word, bigger is the comparison of two things, and biggest is for three or more. Always ask: bigger than what? The word than tells you the thing you are comparing with.
fact: Bigger is the comparative form of big.
fact: «Bigger than» means larger in size or amount than something else.
fact: In maths, the sign > means «is bigger than». The wide end points at the bigger number: 7 > 3.
fact: «The bigger the better» is a phrase that means more is always better.
exercise: «Ben's apple is bigger than Mia's apple.» Whose apple is larger? | Mia's apple | They are exactly the same size | *Ben's apple
exercise: In maths, which sign means «is bigger than»? | < | = | *>
exercise: «My bigger brother is fifteen.» What does bigger mean here? | Quieter | Stronger | Shorter | *Older
test: «This problem is bigger than we thought.» What does bigger mean here? | Smaller and easier | Brighter | *More serious or more important | Less serious
test: Which phrase means that more of something is always better? | Biggest of all the best | *The bigger the better | The bigger the sadder | Bigger than the sky
exam: Which is the comparative form of big? | Biggest | Big | Bigs | *Bigger
